---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Bubble vs window — the dialogue page renderer"
  description="Why some lines pop up in a floating bubble and others fill the bottom dialog box, and the empirical 32-character-per-page cap that bubble text has to fit inside."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Message System', href: `${base}/field-guide/message-system/` },
    { label: 'Bubble vs window' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Tongari Boushi shows dialog in two different shapes. Most NPC conversations
    play in a wide window pinned to the bottom of the dialog screen — the kind
    you'd expect from any DS RPG. But a lot of the game's chatter also pops up
    in a small floating bubble: idle one-liners from NPCs you walk past,
    food-critic verdicts, interaction prompts, and certain talk-topic
    answers depending on the NPC's personality type. Same engine, same
    fonts, very different containers. This page explains how the engine
    decides which one to draw, and why the bubble version has a real
    ceiling on how much English text it can hold.
  </p>

  <h2>What is overlay 157?</h2>
  <p>
    Overlay 157 is the <strong>dialogue page renderer</strong>. Every on-screen
    text bubble and every bottom-dialog text window the player sees in
    conversation is built by code inside this overlay — it lays out lines,
    runs the typewriter animation, picks colors, draws the bubble shape or
    the window border, and tells the sprite hardware where to put each glyph.
    A handful of other game systems live inside the same overlay too (most
    notably the <em>rumor</em> system, the one where you can ask an NPC for
    gossip about another NPC), but the bulk of what ov157 does, every time
    a dialog runs, is render text.
  </p>
  <p>
    Like all overlays, ov157 gets swapped into a shared RAM slot when it's
    needed and unloaded when it isn't. See
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlay-knowledgebase/`}>the
    overlay knowledgebase</a> for the general shape of how overlays load. For
    the byte-level wire format ov157 reads from, see
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/wire-format/`}>the MSG wire format</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Bubble vs window — how the engine picks</h2>
  <p>
    The text itself doesn't choose its container. The bubble-vs-window
    decision is made entirely by <em>whatever piece of game logic asked
    for the dialog</em>, one layer above the renderer. The renderer
    receives a single selector byte from its caller and obeys it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Selector = 0</strong> → window. 260 × 138 pixels, pinned
      to the bottom of the screen. Three lines of text per page, roughly
      thirty characters each.</li>
    <li><strong>Selector = 1 through 4</strong> → bubble. 128 × 76 pixels,
      floating, with a tail pointing at the speaker. Up to two short
      lines per page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>For village residents: personality decides</h3>
  <p>
    Each of the 140 village-resident NPCs (the animal villagers who move
    in and out of your town) has a <strong>personality type</strong> stored
    in the game's NPC data file. There are 12 personality types (6 male,
    6 female), and each personality type maps to its own talk-topic
    overlay — a chunk of game code that handles how that personality's
    conversations play out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two of those 12 personality types have their talk-topic overlays
    coded to use <strong>bubble rendering</strong> for their dialog.
    The other ten use windows. So which shape you see when you chat with
    a villager depends on who they are — specifically, on their
    personality type. You can't change it and the text file doesn't
    control it.
  </p>

  <h3>For special characters and objects: separate paths</h3>
  <p>
    The game's NPC data file also has sections for special/staff NPCs
    (the principal, teachers, shopkeepers, mail carriers), supernatural
    characters (ghosts, gremlins, will-o'-wisps), and legendary creatures
    (Nessie, Unicorn, Dragon, etc.). These characters don't go through
    the personality-based overlay dispatch that villagers use — they have
    their own dialog paths. The rendering mode for their conversations is
    determined by whichever game system triggers their dialog.
  </p>

  <h3>For onemsg floating tips: always bubbles</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>data/onemsg/*.ofs</code> files — the short one-liners NPCs
    say as you walk past them — always render as floating bubbles. They
    enter the engine through a dedicated bubble wrapper that hardcodes
    selector = 4. There is no way for an onemsg entry to render as a
    window.
  </p>

  <h3>Other known bubble paths</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What triggered the dialog</th><th>Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Standard NPC conversation (the usual "talk to someone" flow)</td><td>Window</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Talk-topic answers from bubble-personality villagers</td><td>Bubble</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Talk-topic answers from other villagers</td><td>Window</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Interaction prompts (small confirm bubbles)</td><td>Bubble</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event confirms (e.g. the "you got an item" pop)</td><td>Bubble</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Onemsg floating tips (NPCs you walk past)</td><td>Bubble</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Food-critic verdicts (the reveal bubble at the end of judging)</td><td>Bubble</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The 32-character bubble cap</h2>
  <p>
    The window path has plenty of room — three lines of roughly thirty
    characters each. The bubble path is the tight one, and the bubble
    cap turned out to be one of those translation-side traps that's
    easy to miss until your text starts clipping.
  </p>
  <p>
    Empirically, a single bubble page fits about <strong>32 visible
    characters</strong>, across up to two short lines. We derived that
    number two different ways and they landed on the same answer:
  </p>
  <h3>From the data: 15,083 onemsg phrase segments</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>data/onemsg/*.ofs</code> files hold all the floating-tip
    bubble text (the short things NPCs say as you walk past them). When
    we scanned every visible-character length across all 5,749 entries
    in those files — 15,083 distinct bubble-page segments after splitting
    on page breaks — the distribution had a hard cliff:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`length (chars)  count
    0 -  4      6095
    5 -  9      1506
   10 - 14      2010
   15 - 19      2342
   20 - 24      1972
   25 - 29      1033
   30 - 34       125
   35+             0`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Zero segments above 33 characters. Across more than fifteen thousand
    samples. That's the kind of distribution you get when the people who
    wrote the original Japanese were sizing to a real engine ceiling,
    not just a stylistic preference.
  </p>
  <h3>From the engine: the per-style record at 0x021c336e</h3>
  <p>
    Independently, we read the renderer's own style record out of ov157.
    The bubble's inner text-bounding-box width is 76 pixels (the window's
    is 117), and the font is a 16-pixel proportional font that averages
    about 4.5 pixels per character for typical mixed lowercase Latin
    prose. Two lines per page. That works out to:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`76 px width ÷ ~4.5 px per char ≈ 16-17 chars per line
2 lines per page                ≈ 32-34 chars per page`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Which lines up exactly with the empirical cliff. Bubble pages with
    a lot of wide glyphs (heavy on <code>m</code>, <code>w</code>,
    <code>W</code>, capital letters) will clip a bit sooner, around 28
    chars; pages full of thin glyphs (<code>i</code>, <code>l</code>,
    punctuation) can squeeze closer to 36 before they bind. For
    everyday English we treat <strong>32 characters per page</strong> as
    the safe practical cap, with the 28-32 range flagged as a soft
    warning zone for wide-glyph content.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters for players</h2>
  <p>
    If you spot a bubble where text gets cut off, runs off the bubble
    border, or simply ends mid-word with no continuation — that's almost
    certainly a translation overflow against this 32-character cap. The
    English version of a line that fit comfortably in Japanese can easily
    blow past the limit, because Japanese packs more visible meaning per
    glyph than English does.
  </p>
  <p>
    These cases are real, fixable bugs, and the fix is to rewrite the
    English line to fit. If you see one, please drop a note via the
    <a href={`${base}/bug-reports/`}>bug-reports board</a>, ideally with
    the location (what NPC, what activity, what they were talking about)
    and a screenshot or short description of what was clipped. The
    bubble-cap discovery directly drove a sweep of overflow fixes in
    the v2.4.x patch line, and players catching ones we missed is how
    the long tail gets cleaned up.
  </p>

